  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 131 AM CDT Tue Sep 29 2026\n\nWednesday/Thursday...\n\nThis will be quite the dreary stretch of weather, as we will \ncontinue to see bouts of vorticity flowing into the region ahead of \nthe potent wave over the southern Rockies. There will also be a \nbaroclinic zone that sets up, which will act as a focus for heavier \nrainfall. Although, there is much uncertainty on where this will set \nup at this time. Over the last few forecast runs, this axis has \ntrended south, closer to the Interstate 80 corridor. Although,\nsome guidance still has this north. In either case, we will be \nsaturated with continuous bouts of vorticity passing overhead, \nfavoring widespread rainfall through much of this timeframe. \nOverall, this will generally be in the form of persistent light-\nmoderate rain showers passing through, with heavier rainfall \nexpected at times. While there will be some dry time between \nshowers, we are expecting to remain damp and showery throughout \nmuch of this timeframe. PWATs will be unseasonably high, roughly\nsitting around 2.00\". Thus, if we manage to get more convective\nactivity, then we will have the chance to see heavier rainfall.\nAlthough, there remains some uncertainty on this. \n\nWPC continues to favor much of the area seeing 1.50-3.00\" of \nrain when all is said and done, with localized areas potentially\nseeing 3.00\"+. Confidence on these higher totals is low at this\ntime, with ensemble guidance still in disagreement on amounts \nand location of the heaviest rainfall axis. The Euro suite \nremains the most aggressive with rainfall, highlighting chances \nfor 2.00\"+ of rain by Friday AM around 70-90% roughly along the \nI-80 corridor. Although, the GFS and other ensemble suites are \nfarther north with this axis and only favoring 2.00\"+ with about\n40-70% probabilities. Areas that see the heaviest rainfall will\nhave a low-end flash flood threat, with WPC highlighting our \narea in a Slight Risk, level 2 of 4, for Excessive Rainfall. One\nthing that might limit the flash flood threat will be the \nduration of rainfall. We are not expecting this rainfall to fall\nover a three hour period. Rather, we expect these totals over a\n24-48 hour period. Thus, the long duration should allow for \nmore infiltration than a short duration heavy rainfall event. \nSome rises on area rivers may be possible though, especially \nareas that saw rises with the recent heavy rainfall, along with\nthe potential for some areal flooding.\n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nLate this week and into the weekend, zonal to northwest flow will \nset up over the region. Some guidance hints at a wave passing \nthrough Saturday into Sunday, but moisture will be limited. The \ndrying trend continues from last forecast package, with NBM \nindicating dry conditions through the weekend. Thus, this wave may \njust come through with some passing clouds and a wind shift. \nOtherwise, a cooler and pleasant weekend is currently forecast for \nthe area.",
